---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Tools and profiles — Wombat docs"
  description="Wombat's tool profiles and capabilities: the nine-tool coding profile, tool_search, request_capability, research modes and budgets, web search backends and verification."
  heading="Tools & profiles"
  lede="A tool is a schema in every request. Wombat keeps the default registry small, then lets the model ask for reach when the task needs it."
>
  <section>
    <h2>The registry</h2>
    <p>
      Deduplication cut the always-exposed registry from 34 tools to 18 (19 with
      <code>question</code>). The default <code>coding</code> profile exposes nine of them:
    </p>
    <div class="codewrap">
      <pre><code id="coding-tools">read  write  edit  bash  glob  grep  apply_patch  tool_search  request_capability</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      Git actions run through <code>bash git</code>. Rename, refactor, multi-edit, the separate jobs
      list and the git tools are gone from the registry; the implementations that remain are on a
      one-release soft cut. <code>TestRegistryDedup</code> locks both the 18-tool registry and the
      nine-tool profile, so the count does not drift back up by accident.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Profiles</h2>
    <p>
      A profile is a preset bundle of capabilities. Set it in the config, on
      <code>wombat run --profile</code>, or with <code>/profile</code> in chat.
    </p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">profile</th><th scope="col">capabilities</th><th scope="col">tools</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>minimal</code></td><td>core</td><td>read, write, edit, bash, glob, grep, todowrite, tool_search, request_capability</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>coding</code> (default)</td><td>core + edit</td><td>the nine above plus apply_patch</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>web</code></td><td>core + edit + web</td><td>browser, websearch, webfetch, request_more_research</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>semantic</code></td><td>core + edit + semantic</td><td>lsp</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>verify</code></td><td>core + edit + verify</td><td>verify</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>media</code></td><td>core + edit + media</td><td>imagesearch</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>orchestrator</code></td><td>core + edit</td><td>planning and worker tools, when orchestration is enabled</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>full</code></td><td>everything</td><td>the whole registry, including optional capabilities</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>auto</code></td><td>coding, plus what the prompt implies</td><td>resolved at request time</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Capabilities</h2>
    <p>
      A capability is a named bundle of tools the model can request mid-run. Four are optional and
      can be forced on or off per session.
    </p>

    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">capability</th><th scope="col">loads</th><th scope="col">default</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>web</code></td><td>browser, websearch, webfetch, request_more_research</td><td>auto</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>semantic</code></td><td>lsp — definitions, references, diagnostics, completions, symbols</td><td>off</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>verify</code></td><td>verify — run the project's own tests, build, lint, typecheck</td><td>off</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>media</code></td><td>imagesearch — find and download image references</td><td>off</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>mcp</code></td><td>every tool from your attached MCP servers</td><td>auto</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>How a capability gets loaded</h3>
    <p>
      <code>tool_search</code> takes a query and returns compact matches — name, one-line purpose
      and the tool count — without loading anything. <code>request_capability</code> then loads
      one named capability for the rest of the session.
    </p>
    <div class="codewrap">
      <pre><code id="capability-flow">tools: 8 → schema 1,219 tokens → first request 1,877 input tokens

model: request_capability("web", "need to inspect the page")
result: loaded browser, webfetch, websearch
next request: 11 tools, 2,690 schema tokens</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      That is the whole trade: 1.9k tokens for a coding turn, 2.7k when the browser is genuinely
      needed, instead of ~10k of schemas on every request.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Research budgets</h2>
    <p>
      Research is a policy, not a tool description. Each mode has a soft budget that triggers
      "synthesize now", a hard ceiling that refuses the call, and a wall-clock cap.
    </p>

    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">mode</th><th scope="col">soft search / fetch</th><th scope="col">hard ceiling</th><th scope="col">time cap</th><th scope="col">depth</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>off</code></td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>no web tools</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>quick</code></td><td>2 / 1</td><td>4 / 2</td><td>60s</td><td>fast</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>standard</code></td><td>5 / 3</td><td>8 / 4</td><td>120s</td><td>fast</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>deep</code></td><td>10 / 6</td><td>15 / 8</td><td>300s</td><td>deep</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>auto</code></td><td colspan="4">Quick unless the prompt asks for a deep dive, a comparison, or systematic verification.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      When the soft budget is spent, the tool returns:
    </p>
    <div class="codewrap">
      <pre><code>research fetch soft budget reached (1/1); synthesize now or call
request_more_research with a specific reason</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      <code>request_more_research</code> buys one more search and one more fetch, requires a
      written reason, and is refused once the hard ceiling is hit. This is the difference between
      the old 13-search loop and the 21s research turn measured in the
      <a href="/compare">benchmarks</a>.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <strong>Answer first.</strong> Recommendations and opinions come from the model's knowledge.
      The web is for current facts or explicit verification, and the system prompt says so.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      A vague prompt ("fix it") gets one bounded round: at most two <code>question</code> calls per
      run, each offering 2–3 concrete options, and never a second round. Headless runs —
      <code>wombat run</code> — do not ask at all; they state their assumptions and proceed, then
      exit 0.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Web search and fetch</h2>
    <p>
      <code>websearch</code> runs the configured backends in parallel — Brave, Serper, Exa and
      Tavily when keys are set, plus keyless Bing RSS, DuckDuckGo HTML and Mojeek — then merges,
      cross-checks and ranks. It accepts 2–4 query variants, freshness, language, region and
      pagination; results are cached for ten minutes; trusted docs and GitHub are boosted and
      SEO spam is filtered. By default it also fetches the top three pages and adds an excerpt
      with date and author when it can find them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>webfetch</code> extracts the main article text, pretty-prints JSON, returns XML as
      text, and says so plainly for PDFs and binary payloads. Both share a 15-second HTTP client
      with retries and a five-second grace window after the first backend answers.
    </p>
    <div class="codewrap">
      <pre><code id="search-keys">export EXA_API_KEY=...
export TAVILY_API_KEY=...
export BRAVE_API_KEY=...
export SERPER_API_KEY=...</code></pre>
      <button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="search-keys">copy</button>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Verification and semantics</h2>
    <p>
      <code>verify</code> reads the project, works out the test, build, lint and typecheck
      commands, and runs them. With <code>require_verification</code> on, a run cannot report
      success without a passing check. Writers always run their checks before a patch merges.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>lsp</code> asks a language server for definitions, references, diagnostics,
      completions, workspace symbols and file outlines. Go uses <code>gopls</code>; TypeScript,
      Python, Rust, C/C++, Java, Ruby, PHP, Lua, C#, Swift, Kotlin and shell use their standard
      servers when installed. Set <code>WOMBAT_LSP_&lt;EXT&gt;</code> to point at a specific
      binary.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
